#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95e51046-fc4c-536b-91eb-82c7c298b7be" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Product as a Repeated Addition"}] :question/id #uuid "a3241956-5b5b-5760-9383-d7da13f48b16" :question/math-academy-id "e-10548" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fd479debb08b47c49552e543d2a8a53d0cdcbfb59c9a43333b7650f33bd063a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Write down this multiplication as a repeated addition of fraction models." :question/worked-solution "In the model, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}\n$$\n\nmultiplied by ${\\color{blue}3}$. This means that we need to make $\\color{blue}3$ copies of the given fraction model.\n\n![](images/38/38d0f24ab003913a30051d64dda233980173939825d9cc6f34473b1cbdcdbeed.png)\n\nSo, the missing fraction model is:\n\n![](images/ac/ac8618a503eb2cfd41a7fa8851cdb0dbb7152325100ec922b533d9da6e63b7c5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e7017e-45df-5adf-9ded-3f815b4f5673" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Unit Fraction by a Whole Number Using Repeated Addition"}] :question/id #uuid "eea25500-f78e-544f-8bf9-7eaacfac84a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10549" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5aef67e5f151465f09eba64456f4566cc984c97857d8e6203cd25d60952e183a.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5}$." :question/worked-solution "Since we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{5}\n$$\n\nby $\\color{blue}3$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe41721739d82df825d2a7e28bc19590fbc784354a58aa74137aceda0e6be202.png)\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 3\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23f5f4f6-9d8c-5ab5-ada8-1212832de40f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Multiplication Problem Given a Model"}] :question/id #uuid "1c074473-dc1c-56c6-825b-be4b6b3dbfa2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10550" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e71724d5a2f16bd88fdae52e2717ec65176ff82bc74e92cd7e18a2a19f1b8218.png)\n\nWhat multiplication problem is represented by the model above?" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/ee/eef3cec4311b03e7f4be59e367cbd3d6947afd73b16a00b487f28ae7ea2bf8b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\times 4 = \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can swap the factors on the left-hand side. So, the following would be a correct answer too:\n\n$$\n4 \\times \\dfrac{1}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bf2fa4e-c47e-5d89-a4aa-8e255050f792" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Missing Factor Given a Model"}] :question/id #uuid "3e10607a-5812-50cd-a2d5-4b7c3ebcbde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10555" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e9bc9f6b014b092a363dbac6ff64bedefe26846c34851fd5029c89af61d68d8f.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the multiplication problem above?" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/c1/c1efa667a938907fcdd5ba9d0172b37a7efb0a7b312e3025556c4444814d2ab3.png)\n\nThe shape on the left has $1$ shaded piece, and the shape on the right has $3$ shaded pieces. So the number of shaded pieces has increased by a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\times {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e7017e-45df-5adf-9ded-3f815b4f5673" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Unit Fraction by a Whole Number Using Repeated Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dedca14b-55aa-5bd3-b33d-7ae7b09edbe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070122} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e78012-6a71-5363-8248-a523ed8b7c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070123} {:answer/id #uuid "05addd86-50e5-5b12-91f5-a514a7f1d0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070124} {:answer/id #uuid "5534213d-ce85-5e69-be2f-970a54c2f162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070125} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef4034d-5fd8-5ab7-b554-c26fea04cb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070126}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e7271485-550a-5ab5-942e-b4a20d6e2b8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070121}] :question/id #uuid "a42b8381-bd96-5ac6-8621-6c737c278608" :question/math-academy-id "q-136849" :question/problem "![](images/60/607bb957bbf20006ed9730d94f4a42a724d88ee64a6ab9f01b952c9e2cebe19e.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bf2fa4e-c47e-5d89-a4aa-8e255050f792" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Missing Factor Given a Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5701a88-053a-52b0-b26a-9abaf0c563b1" :answer/type {:db/id #e